const yogas = [
  {
    title: "Withdrawal and concentration",
    body:
      "The practitioner withdraws ordinary sensory fixation and learns to stabilize awareness around increasingly subtle luminous signs.",
  },
  {
    title: "Vital wind discipline",
    body:
      "Subtle winds are guided through the central channel through advanced breath and posture disciplines.",
  },
  {
    title: "Retention and recollection",
    body:
      "Conceptual turbulence is stilled while the practitioner develops the empty-form dimensions of deity realization.",
  },
  {
    title: "Meditative stabilization",
    body:
      "Practice matures into immutable great bliss and direct realization of the clear-light dimension of awakening.",
  },
];

const preliminaries = [
  "Refuge and prostrations",
  "Bodhicitta and intention training",
  "Vajrasattva purification",
  "Mandala offerings and guru yoga",
  "Generation-stage deity practice as support for completion stage",
];
---

<div class="page-stack">
  <section class="editorial-intro">
    <div>
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Kalachakra in Jonang</span>
      <h2>The Wheel of Time as the tradition's deepest practical inheritance</h2>
      <p class="editorial-lede">
        Many Tibetan schools transmit Kalachakra, but Jonang is widely regarded
        as the principal holder of the complete Dro lineage and its completion-stage
        yogas. This is one of the core reasons the school remains so important.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Jonang, Kalachakra is not merely a ceremonial empowerment or a
        symbolic mandala. It is a whole contemplative architecture that relates
        the cosmos, the subtle body, meditative realization, and the path to
        awakening.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="editorial-panel">
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Important Boundary</span>
      <p>
        This page is an introduction, not a practice manual. The completion
        stage is traditionally entered only through empowerment, transmission,
        instruction, and retreat under qualified guidance.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="editorial-grid--two items-start">
    <div>
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Why Kalachakra Matters</span>
      <h2>A system linking body, world, and awakening</h2>
      <p>
        Kalachakra works with correspondences between outer cycles and inner
        experience. The body is not treated as incidental to realization. The
        subtle channels, winds, and drops become part of the path, and the
        practitioner learns to align contemplative transformation with a wider
        vision of time, cosmos, and enlightened embodiment.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is also why the tradition intersects so naturally with Tibetan
        medicine and astrology: the same body-world mapping that serves tantric
        practice also informs broader understandings of balance, energy, and
        psycho-physical cultivation.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="editorial-panel editorial-panel--accent">
      <span class="editorial-kicker text-white/70">Jonang emphasis</span>
      <p class="text-white/88">
        Jonang is especially known for preserving not only the symbolic and
        ritual dimensions of Kalachakra, but the demanding completion-stage
        yogas that require retreat, discipline, and a long training arc.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="editorial-kicker">Preparation</span>
    <h2>The path begins with formation, not immediacy</h2>
    <p>
      Before a practitioner enters the deeper yogas, training begins with common
      and uncommon preliminaries. These practices orient intention, purify
      obscurations, establish devotion and stability, and prepare the mind-body
      system for more subtle work.
    </p>
    <ul class="editorial-checklist">
      {preliminaries.map((item) => (
        <li>{item}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="editorial-kicker">Six Vajra Yogas</span>
    <h2>The completion-stage arc in approachable terms</h2>
    <div class="editorial-grid md:grid-cols-2">
      {yogas.map((yoga) => (
        <article class="editorial-card">
          <h3>{yoga.title}</h3>
          <p>{yoga.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p>
      Traditional retreat structures can stretch across years. Jonang sources
      describe both rigorous sequence and the importance of signs of
      realization, rather than treating practice as a checklist to be rushed
      through.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="editorial-grid--two items-start">
    <div class="editorial-panel">
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Dark Retreat and Isolation</span>
      <p>
        Jonang descriptions of practice often emphasize dark retreat and the
        three isolations of body, speech, and mind. These settings reduce
        ordinary sensory momentum so that subtler perceptions and meditative
        signs can become workable.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="editorial-panel">
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Medicine and Cosmology</span>
      <p>
        Because Kalachakra maps macrocosm and microcosm together, it also
        intersects with Tibetan medical and astrological sciences. Practice and
        physiology are not separate conceptual worlds here.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="editorial-panel">
    <span class="editorial-kicker">What This Page Offers</span>
    <h2>An orientation to a guarded tradition</h2>
    <p>
      The value of a public introduction is to clarify why Kalachakra matters to
      Jonang identity and why the lineage is treated with such seriousness. The
      point is not access without preparation, but understanding why this
      training remains one of the school's defining inheritances.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
